\begin{theorem}[Vanishing of horizontal meridians]\hypertarget{tag-02RG}{}\label{night:P2.6}\label{stmt:P2.6}
\StatementRecord{night:P2.6}{proved}{02RG}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}

In a smooth resolved proper filling carrying the marked base map, every parameter-central component dominating $\Pp^1$ has zero normal-meridian lattice class modulo $\Z\widehat\delta$, relative to the ordinary parameter origin.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item Put $B^\circ=\Pp^1\setminus\{0,1,\infty\}$. After $r=R^n$, the quotient fundamental group of the punctured family over $B^\circ$ by the normal subgroup generated by $\widehat\delta$ is
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01OB}{}\label{eq:P2split}
 \bigl((\Lambda/\Z\widehat\delta)\rtimes\pi_1(B^\circ)\bigr)\times\Z_R.
\end{equation}
The finite Jacobian linear changes are independent of $c$ and fix $\widehat\delta$. The $R$ descent is the $\delta$ translation $H$, all its branch changes are integral $\delta$ periods, and its lattice conjugation differs from the identity by a $\delta$ multiple. The finite gluing changes under $c\mapsto c-12n$ also lie in $\Z\delta$. Thus every cross-cocycle disappears in exactly the quotient \eqref{eq:P2split}; this proves the presentation from the actual chart action.

\item Let $D$ dominate $B$. Its positive normal circle has parameter degree $e=\ord_D(R)>0$, and in \eqref{eq:P2split} is $(\lambda,e)$ with trivial base-loop coordinate. Changing a basing path by a fibre loop changes $\lambda$ by $(I-T_R^e)$ of that loop, which vanishes modulo $\delta$. Transport along $D$ therefore acts on $\lambda$ only by base monodromy.

\item The proper map $D\to B$ has Stein factorization, in the precise analytic form of \cite[Theorem 8.5]{Movasati24}: a proper holomorphic map factors into a map with connected fibres and a finite map. Here $D$ is compact smooth and $B$ a smooth curve, so the hypotheses hold. Remove finitely many branch and critical values. The first map is then a proper submersion with connected fibres; path lifting and a path in a fibre show surjectivity on fundamental groups. The finite curve map has finite-index image in the punctured base group. Removing the other divisors from $D$ preserves this image, since loops in a smooth complex manifold can be moved away from a proper complex analytic subset of real codimension at least two. Hence a finite-index subgroup is represented by paths that transport the normal meridian.

\item The orbit of $\lambda$ is finite. \ref{night:P2.5} kills it rationally, and the quotient lattice is free, so it vanishes integrally. This includes every multiplicity $e$. After a local finite root in the base, each horizontal component inherits this zero class from its global preimage; we do not assume that the local component itself is global.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

